## Coffin Nails vs Ballerina Nails: Is There Actually a Difference?

No. They are the same shape. Full stop.

This is worth saying plainly because the confusion is real and persistent  "ballerina or coffin, same thing right?" is one of the most searched questions in this space, and most guides dance around it rather than just answering. Per the professional glossary at <a href='https://nailknowledge.org/glossary/coffin-nails' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>NailKnowledge</a>, coffin and ballerina are regional and generational name variants for one identical shape. Coffin became dominant in North America; ballerina picked up traction in the UK and Europe. Neither is more correct.

In a salon, either name will be understood. If you want to be extra clear, bring a reference photo. But you will not get a different shape based on which name you use  there is no version of "ballerina" that is softer or more rounded than "coffin." They describe the same tapered-sides, flat-tip silhouette.

The shape that is often confused with coffin is almond  which also has tapered sides but ends in a rounded point rather than a flat cut. If you want a full breakdown of how they differ in practice, the <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/almond-nails-guide'>almond nails guide</a> runs through the comparison in detail, and the dedicated <a href='/blog/coffin-nails/coffin-nails-vs-almond-nails'>coffin vs almond</a> post covers which shape suits which hand type and lifestyle.

