## How to Tell if Coffin Nails Will Suit Your Hands

If you've ever wondered whether coffin nails would actually work on your hands, the hesitation usually comes from one of two places: short fingers or wide nail beds. Both concerns are more manageable than most guides suggest.

**Short fingers:** The coffin shape's tapered sides create a visual narrowing and elongating effect that is genuinely useful on shorter fingers. The key is length. A very short coffin barely has room to read as coffin  it risks looking like a cut-down square. Move to a medium length (roughly 3–5mm of free edge past the fingertip) and the shape starts to pull the eye along the nail, giving the finger the appearance of more length. This is the same principle as a pointed toe  the eye follows the line.

**Wide nail beds:** Coffin is actually one of the better shape choices here. The tapering on both sides visually narrows the plate in a way that round or square shapes cannot. The flat tip keeps the look graphic rather than pointed-aggressive. What to avoid on wide nail beds is a very short coffin  the shape doesn't have enough length to do its narrowing work, and the width reads more prominently as a result.

**Practical considerations:** Coffin is structurally more demanding than square or oval because the stress concentrates at the corners of the flat tip. Longer sets are more prone to breakage at those corners, particularly if the C-curve isn't properly built in. If you're new to extended lengths or have naturally brittle nails, start at a shorter or medium coffin length and build up if you want to go longer.

