## What to Say at the Salon to Get the Exact Coffin Shape You Want

A reference photo is worth more than any technical vocabulary. Bring one.

That said, knowing how to communicate shape specifics makes the conversation faster and the result more reliable. When asking for coffin nails, the variables your tech needs to know are: length (short, medium, or long  or a specific millimetre measurement if you know it), material (acrylic, builder gel, or natural nail shaping), and C-curve preference (standard or pronounced). If your previous set had corners that kept breaking, say so  your tech will compensate by building more material at the stress points or suggesting a shorter length.

The phrase "my nail tech keeps making them too square" is one of the most common complaints in coffin nail conversations. It usually means the sides weren't filed inward enough before the tip was flattened  the shape sat between coffin and square rather than committing to either. The fix is to specify: "tapered sides, not straight sides  I want them to narrow before the tip." Combined with a photo, this removes the ambiguity.

For the nail bed width question: if you have wider nail beds and want the coffin shape to look proportional, ask your tech to taper more aggressively on the sides. This narrows the visual width of the plate and makes the flat tip look sharp rather than wide.

The salon communication anxiety  the "I'm not sure how to describe what I want" hesitation  is worth addressing directly: the nail industry is extremely visual. Techs work from reference photos constantly. You do not need the technical vocabulary. You need a clear image of what you're aiming for and the ability to say what you didn't like about your last set.

